{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"**Pushed.** `origin/main` is now at `44bc186`, and PR #4 is a single commit against it — the diff is isolated to this work (`package-lock` now reads `0.18.0 → 0.19.0` instead of `0.1.0 → 0.19.0`). GitHub had cached the old merge base, so I closed and reopened the PR to force it to recompute; same PR number and body.\n\nOne thing from that push worth noting: your pre-push hook held the ledger back with potential-secret findings (\"Run `cledger sync` to review and remediate\"). That's pre-existing — those events were captured by your installed 0.18.0, not by this branch — but it's directly relevant to your second question, below.\n\n## On smoke-testing with a real install\n\n**Yes, worth it — but a narrow test, not a broad one.** What I ran was real transcripts (Claude, codex, opencode) replayed through `cledger capture` into scratch repos, which exercises conversion, append, redaction and scan on genuine data. Three things that path *cannot* reach:\n\n**1. The live hook, with file-history timing.** My tests read complete transcript files. In a live session the Stop hook fires while Claude Code is still writing, and file-history backups are flushed around the same time as the transcript line that names them. If the hook wins that race, digests silently come back empty — the event is still correct, just less useful. I resolved digests fine on historical files; never on a live one. This is the single most likely thing to behave differently.\n\n**2. Whether the scan surface got noisier.** Capturing `system` stdout, queued prompts, and attachments means more raw text now flows through the secret scanner. Given your ledger is *already* being held back on findings, more input could mean more gating. Worth knowing before it surprises you mid-push.\n\n**3. Volume, in your judgement rather than mine.** Measured on one real 208-turn session: turns are 981 KB, and everything new adds 154 KB — about 16% overhead, with `context_injection` the largest slice at 61 KB. I'd call that cheap for what it buys, but you may disagree about specific types: `deferred_tools_delta` and `agent_listing_delta` attachments are pure harness boilerplate that repeats verbatim across sessions and is reconstructible from the CLI version. I deliberately kept them (your brief was \"everything\"), but they're the obvious candidates if you want to trim.\n\nThe opencode subagent recursion *is* covered — my test session spawned a `task` subagent and capture walked into it, 38 events. Codex sub-agent rollouts remain unverified; that's in the roadmap.\n\nConcretely, in a throwaway repo:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/capture-everything && npm link\ncd /tmp/scratch-repo && cledger install claude-code\n# run one real session that edits a file and spawns a subagent, then:\ncledger log --with-state | head -40\ncledger export | jq -c 'select(.kind==\"file_snapshot\") | {seq: .conversation.seq, resolved}'   # digests present?\ncledger scan                                                                                   # new findings?\n```\n\nIf the digests come back null on a live session, that's a real bug and I'd fix it by having the hook retry the backup read; if the volume or scan noise bothers you, say which types and I'll add the filter. Want me to hold here for your results, or go ahead and pre-emptively add a retry on the digest read?","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-capture-everything","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/capture-everything","head":"1100858ed17a7ee0b377097e93eafa24d3638f68","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:9a01c126-8f0a-4060-95f9-fe3a00a2a8c3","seq":744},"id":"ev1-f82bd7272a6b74a692a3e4aa8058be8abdf54ed42ca3e39cca8735f119489dcd","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-04T04:21:19.592Z","producer":{