{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Everything below is verified against your actual setup. Two facts make this simple: your global `cledger` is already a symlink into the main checkout, and the Claude Code hook is already installed globally. So testing is just *repointing one symlink* — no `npm link`, no build, no sudo, no hook install.\n\n**One-line blast radius:** while the symlink is swapped, every Claude/codex session anywhere on your machine captures with the new code. It's append-only and safe, just not scoped. Step 6 puts it back.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1 — point `cledger` at the branch\n\n```sh\nln -sfn /Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/capture-everything /opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/conversation-ledger && cledger --version\n```\n\nExpect: `0.19.0`\n\n### Step 2 — make a scratch repo\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p ~/cledger-test && cd ~/cledger-test && git init -q . && echo \"# scratch\" > README.md && git add -A && git commit -qm init && pwd\n```\n\n### Step 3 — save the check script (paste the whole block)\n\n```sh\ncat > ~/cledger-check.sh <<'SCRIPT'\n#!/bin/sh\necho \"── 1. what got captured ──\"\ncledger export 2>/dev/null | jq -r .kind | sort | uniq -c\necho \"\"\necho \"── 2. file digests (the live-timing check) ──\"\ncledger export 2>/dev/null | jq -r '\n  select(.kind==\"file_snapshot\")\n  | { named:    ([.content.files[]? | select(.backup_file != null)] | length),\n      resolved: ((.resolved.files? // []) | length) }\n  | if   .named == 0         then \"no backups named (normal)\"\n    elif .resolved >= .named then \"digests resolved (GOOD)\"\n    else                          \"MISSING DIGESTS (tell Claude)\"\n    end' | sort | uniq -c\necho \"\"\necho \"── 3. subagent sub-conversations ──\"\ncledger conversations --all 2>/dev/null | grep '#agent:' || echo \"  (none — did a subagent actually run?)\"\necho \"\"\necho \"── 4. secret scan ──\"\ncledger scan 2>&1 | tail -2\nSCRIPT\necho saved\n```\n\n### Step 4 — run a real session\n\n```sh\ncd ~/cledger-test && claude\n```\n\nPaste this as your prompt, let it finish, then type `/exit`:\n\n> Create notes.md with three bullet points about git, then edit it to add a fourth. Then use the Explore agent to summarize what's in this repo.\n\n(That prompt is chosen to force a file edit *and* a subagent — the two paths I couldn't test offline.)\n\n### Step 5 — check the results\n\n```sh\ncd ~/cledger-test && sh ~/cledger-check.sh\n```\n\nWhat good looks like:\n\n| Section | Good | Bad → tell me |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | five or six kinds listed, `conversation_turn` the largest | only `conversation_turn` (new code didn't run) |\n| 2 | `digests resolved (GOOD)`, possibly some `no backups named (normal)` | any `MISSING DIGESTS` |\n| 3 | a line containing `#agent:` | `(none)` even though a subagent ran |\n| 4 | `no findings` | a finding count — paste the count only, **not** the content |\n\n### Step 6 — put it back (do this when done)\n\n```sh\nln -sfn /Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger /opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/conversation-ledger && cledger --version\n```\n\nExpect: `0.18.0`. Cleanup if you want it: `rm -rf ~/cledger-test ~/cledger-check.sh`.\n\n---\n\n**Two findings while building this.** First, my earlier \"2 empty digests\" reading was a false alarm — those were snapshots with no tracked files at all, which is normal; the check script above now tells the two cases apart. Second, I checked whether the new capture types widen the secret-scan surface: the 12 findings in my scratch repo sit on ordinary opencode `conversation_turn` events, which 0.18.0 captured identically. So this change didn't introduce them — I looked at event kinds only, not content.\n\nOne aftereffect to know about: events captured during the test window carry the new kinds, so after Step 6 your 0.18.0 `cledger log` will show session-machinery events inline (it has no `--with-state` filter to hide them). Merg